authors:
  - family-names: Kline
    given-names: Jeffery
repository-code: "https://github.com/jeff-kline/modular-hadamard-large-orders"
url: "https://github.com/jeff-kline/modular-hadamard-large-orders"
license: GPL-3.0-only
abstract: >-
  An m-modular Hadamard matrix of order n is an n x n matrix H with entries
  ±1 and H H^T = n I modulo m. The paper shows that 7-modular Hadamard
  matrices exist at every order divisible by 4, answering Problem 11 of the
  2026 survey of Egan, Kotsireas, London and Ó Catháin, and that 11- and
  13-modular Hadamard matrices exist at every order divisible by 4, each
  using one Hadamard matrix announced in 2026. At all sufficiently large
  orders divisible by 4 it gives m-modular Hadamard matrices for every odd
  prime, every power of 2, and every m of the form 4p^a or 4pq, including
  m = 140; for every m it gives them at all large orders divisible by a
  power of 2 depending on m. It also proves Kuperberg's Conjecture 5.1, with
  0 counted as a square, for every odd prime p not congruent to 1 mod 8. The
  proofs use the direct-sum lemma of Lee–Szőllősi and Kuperberg with
  complements of projective geometries and Brouwer's symmetric designs. The
  repository checks exact instances of several of the constructions; the
  checks do not replace the proofs. Novelty is
  bounded to the sources searched.
keywords:
  - Hadamard matrices
  - modular Hadamard matrices
  - symmetric designs
  - projective geometry
